1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,279
Take four labels: a, b, c and d. Join them in a branching tree with no root.

2
00:00:06,279 --> 00:00:13,220
Its middle edge separates two pairs. Here, a and b are together on one side, while c

3
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:15,120
and d are on the other.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,236
There are only three possible pairings.

5
00:00:18,236 --> 00:00:21,532
Another tree might group a with c, or a with d.

6
00:00:21,532 --> 00:00:24,970
This four-label relationship is called a quartet.

7
00:00:24,970 --> 00:00:30,020
Agreement means the same pairing, not simply using the same four labels.

8
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:34,741
Now imagine three trees describing the same larger set of species.

9
00:00:34,741 --> 00:00:38,951
Must some four species have the same pairing in all three trees?

10
00:00:38,951 --> 00:00:41,714
The answer becomes yes at fourteen leaves.

11
00:00:41,714 --> 00:00:44,608
At thirteen, agreement can still be avoided.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,527
A leaf is a tip of the tree. Every internal junction has three branches, and no point

13
00:00:50,527 --> 00:00:56,640
is designated as an ancestor or root. To inspect four chosen leaves, keep the paths connecting

14
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:58,656
them and erase everything else.

15
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:02,357
Then straighten any points that merely sit along a path.

16
00:01:02,357 --> 00:01:05,265
The remaining shape is one of our three quartets.

17
00:01:05,265 --> 00:01:07,936
Edge lengths in these drawings do not matter.

18
00:01:07,936 --> 00:01:11,081
The pairing depends only on how the branches connect.

19
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:14,637
Here is the actual thirteen-leaf example used in the paper.

20
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:19,418
Its labels are one through thirteen. We will keep the branches fixed and move the labels.

21
00:01:19,418 --> 00:01:24,093
That preserves the unlabelled shape while changing the relationships between particular

22
00:01:24,093 --> 00:01:24,523
species.

23
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:29,324
Rotate the labels one, two and three around their three positions.

24
00:01:29,324 --> 00:01:34,687
Do the same within four, five, six; seven, eight, nine; and ten, eleven, twelve.

25
00:01:34,687 --> 00:01:40,452
Thirteen stays fixed. One rotation gives the second tree, and another gives the third.

26
00:01:40,833 --> 00:01:44,735
Look only at labels one, four, seven and thirteen.

27
00:01:44,735 --> 00:01:48,480
In the first tree, four and seven form one pair.

28
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:52,928
In the other two trees, one and four form a pair instead.

29
00:01:52,928 --> 00:01:55,737
The three answers are not all equal.

30
00:01:56,133 --> 00:02:02,341
Notice that two answers can match. We only need to prevent agreement by all three trees

31
00:02:02,341 --> 00:02:08,833
at once. In fact, each pair of these trees agrees on one hundred and thirty-seven quartets.

32
00:02:08,833 --> 00:02:11,901
Pairwise agreement is a different question.

33
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:16,735
Thirteen leaves contain seven hundred and fifteen groups of four.

34
00:02:16,735 --> 00:02:21,648
The check examines every group, and finds none with three equal answers.

35
00:02:21,648 --> 00:02:26,015
Two methods, using path distances and edge splits, confirm this.

36
00:02:26,015 --> 00:02:29,700
It is an exhaustive finite check, not random sampling.

37
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:34,473
One example is enough to show that thirteen cannot force agreement.

38
00:02:34,473 --> 00:02:37,672
But proving that fourteen always works is harder.

39
00:02:37,672 --> 00:02:43,220
We must cover every possible triple of trees, not just trees built by our relabelling

40
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:43,612
trick.

41
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,602
The useful object is a separating edge.

42
00:02:46,602 --> 00:02:49,537
Cutting it divides the leaves into two sets.

43
00:02:49,537 --> 00:02:52,806
Pick two leaves on one side and two on the other.

44
00:02:52,806 --> 00:02:56,408
The quartet must pair the two from each side together.

45
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:02,493
A standard balanced-edge fact says that some edge leaves at least one third of the leaves

46
00:03:02,493 --> 00:03:07,929
on either side. With fourteen leaves, each side must therefore contain at least five.

47
00:03:07,929 --> 00:03:10,168
Colour the two sides blue and teal.

48
00:03:10,567 --> 00:03:13,722
Carry those same colours into the second tree.

49
00:03:13,722 --> 00:03:19,896
An exhaustive check shows that a suitable edge always preserves three leaves of one colour

50
00:03:19,896 --> 00:03:23,463
opposite four of the other. Keep those seven leaves.

51
00:03:23,463 --> 00:03:27,031
The two colour sets are now separated in both trees.

52
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:32,816
The finite check covers all one hundred and thirty-five unlabelled shapes, and every

53
00:03:32,816 --> 00:03:35,783
colouring with five, six or seven blue leaves.

54
00:03:35,783 --> 00:03:41,457
That is one million, one hundred and thirty-eight thousand, nine hundred and ninety-five

55
00:03:41,457 --> 00:03:43,456
cases. There are zero failures.

56
00:03:43,833 --> 00:03:47,123
Now restrict the third tree to the seven leaves we kept.

57
00:03:47,123 --> 00:03:49,943
A balanced edge has at least three on each side.

58
00:03:49,943 --> 00:03:54,878
Put the leaves into a grid: rows are their old colours, and columns are the sides of

59
00:03:54,878 --> 00:03:55,641
this new cut.

60
00:03:56,033 --> 00:04:01,842
In this illustrative grid, the top row contains three leaves and the bottom row four.

61
00:04:01,842 --> 00:04:07,788
Each column also has at least three. The two diagonal cells contain two blue leaves and

62
00:04:07,788 --> 00:04:10,385
three teal leaves. Keep two from each.

63
00:04:10,767 --> 00:04:14,793
They are opposite colours, so the first two trees separate them.

64
00:04:14,793 --> 00:04:19,889
They are also on opposite sides of the new cut, so the third tree separates them.

65
00:04:19,889 --> 00:04:23,727
All three trees must therefore give the same quartet pairing.

66
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:29,942
Why must a usable diagonal exist? If both diagonals failed, each would contain a cell

67
00:04:29,942 --> 00:04:34,685
with at most one leaf. Those two small cells share a row or a column.

68
00:04:34,685 --> 00:04:40,252
That row or column would contain at most two, contradicting our minimum of three.

69
00:04:40,633 --> 00:04:46,104
We have both halves of the threshold. Thirteen admits a counterexample, and fourteen

70
00:04:46,104 --> 00:04:51,770
forces agreement. Write h of three for the smallest forcing number: it equals fourteen.

71
00:04:51,770 --> 00:04:56,785
Larger sets also work, because you can first restrict to any fourteen leaves.

72
00:04:57,167 --> 00:05:02,436
For many trees, we can repeat the idea: keep two separated sets while processing one

73
00:05:02,436 --> 00:05:06,074
tree after another. The challenge is that the sets shrink.

74
00:05:06,074 --> 00:05:10,967
We need to track enough information to ensure two leaves survive on each side.

75
00:05:11,367 --> 00:05:18,037
Call their sizes a and d. Define a budget as the smallest of three numbers: a times d,

76
00:05:18,037 --> 00:05:24,941
twice a squared, and twice d squared. A budget greater than two forces both integer sizes

77
00:05:24,941 --> 00:05:26,415
to be at least two.

78
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:29,970
Take sixteen blue leaves and thirty-two teal leaves.

79
00:05:29,970 --> 00:05:35,152
The three budget candidates are five hundred and twelve, five hundred and twelve, and

80
00:05:35,152 --> 00:05:39,481
two thousand and forty-eight. Their minimum is five hundred and twelve.

81
00:05:39,481 --> 00:05:42,712
Now cut the next tree, balancing the larger teal set.

82
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:48,897
Suppose the cut divides blue into seven and nine, and teal into eleven and twenty-one.

83
00:05:48,897 --> 00:05:53,211
Either diagonal remains separated in all trees processed so far.

84
00:05:53,211 --> 00:05:56,986
The seven and twenty-one choice has budget ninety-eight.

85
00:05:56,986 --> 00:06:00,356
The nine and eleven choice has budget ninety-nine.

86
00:06:00,733 --> 00:06:03,226
Choose the larger budget, ninety-nine.

87
00:06:03,226 --> 00:06:09,128
The proof guarantees a retained fraction c, about eighteen point eight five eight percent.

88
00:06:09,128 --> 00:06:14,179
Multiplying the old budget by c gives about ninety-six point five five three.

89
00:06:14,179 --> 00:06:16,933
Our chosen diagonal clears that guarantee.

90
00:06:17,333 --> 00:06:21,914
Keep naming the smaller retained set X, and the larger Y.

91
00:06:21,914 --> 00:06:28,906
On the next tree, balance Y again. For the algebra, x and y mean the fractions of X and

92
00:06:28,906 --> 00:06:31,397
Y lying on one side of the cut.

93
00:06:31,767 --> 00:06:36,088
The proof rescales the sizes so the smaller is one.

94
00:06:36,088 --> 00:06:41,511
Larger size ratios can be reduced to two for a worst-case bound.

95
00:06:41,511 --> 00:06:46,935
Swapping the cut's sides lets us assume x plus y is at most one.

96
00:06:47,333 --> 00:06:52,629
The two cases meet where x squared equals one minus x, divided by three.

97
00:06:52,629 --> 00:06:58,587
Call that crossing t. Above t the first diagonal works; below t the second works.

98
00:06:58,587 --> 00:07:04,397
Checking all three budget terms proves the shared guarantee c equals t squared.

99
00:07:04,767 --> 00:07:10,532
This constant is best possible for this real-number inequality, not for every tree strategy.

100
00:07:10,532 --> 00:07:13,665
Its written proof has been independently reviewed.

101
00:07:13,665 --> 00:07:17,612
Lean also checks the sharp inequality and its equality example.

102
00:07:17,612 --> 00:07:23,127
The tree model, iteration, finite searches and fourteen-leaf theorem are not formalised.

103
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:26,089
Repeat the budget step for each new tree.

104
00:07:26,089 --> 00:07:30,763
The initial budget is at least two ninths of the square of the leaf count.

105
00:07:30,763 --> 00:07:35,562
Requiring the final budget to stay above two gives this general upper bound.

106
00:07:35,562 --> 00:07:39,604
Its exponential base, beta, is about two point three zero three.

107
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,579
For one through six trees, this formula gives the guarantees shown.

108
00:07:44,579 --> 00:07:50,320
They need not be exact: for three trees it gives sixteen, while the sharper argument

109
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:56,608
gives fourteen. Exact finite bookkeeping improves the four-tree upper bound to twenty-eight.

110
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,242
On the lower side, four explicit trees on twenty-two leaves have no common quartet.

111
00:08:02,242 --> 00:08:07,042
Checking all seven thousand, three hundred and fifteen groups confirms this.

112
00:08:07,042 --> 00:08:11,463
So the four-tree threshold lies between twenty-three and twenty-eight.

113
00:08:11,463 --> 00:08:13,800
Its exact value remains unknown here.

114
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:20,717
For any number k of trees, the lower bound two to the k plus two was already stated by

115
00:08:20,717 --> 00:08:25,340
Snir and Yuster. The paper supplies a construction and proof.

116
00:08:25,340 --> 00:08:32,464
The contribution here includes the new general upper bound and the exact three-tree threshold.

117
00:08:32,833 --> 00:08:36,547
Keep three ideas. A quartet is a pairing of four leaves.

118
00:08:36,547 --> 00:08:40,394
Separated sets preserve that pairing across several trees.

119
00:08:40,394 --> 00:08:44,970
And a shrinking budget lets the argument continue through many trees.

120
00:08:44,970 --> 00:08:50,674
The result concerns guaranteed local agreement, not overall similarity of evolutionary

121
00:08:50,674 --> 00:08:51,337
histories.

122
00:08:51,733 --> 00:08:57,218
Correctness confidence is high. The novelty assessment rests on a bounded literature

123
00:08:57,218 --> 00:09:00,221
search, not proof that no earlier work exists.

124
00:09:00,221 --> 00:09:05,770
Exploratory searches on twenty-three leaves remain unresolved and provide no evidence

125
00:09:05,770 --> 00:09:07,141
for a stronger bound.

126
00:09:07,533 --> 00:09:13,365
The full paper, explicit trees and verification files accompany this explanation.

127
00:09:13,365 --> 00:09:19,485
The video shows the mechanism and its limits; it does not reproduce every case of the

128
00:09:19,485 --> 00:09:22,509
computational proof. Valyu Research, 2026.

129
00:09:22,509 --> 00:09:24,885
Narration uses a synthetic voice.
